@startuml
' What one chat request looks like on the wire, and where the two cache breakpoints sit.
' Everything above a breakpoint is charged once (write, 1.25×) and read back cheaply (0.1×)
' on the next request — as long as the bytes are identical. Render: see diagrams/render.sh
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #33475B
  FontColor #12243A
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFF8DC
  BorderColor #C8B560
}

rectangle "One request to the Messages API (sent again every round)" as req {
  rectangle "1. tools — six JSON schemas\nget_plan, get_days, get_calendar, get_focus_sessions,\npropose_week, propose_log" as t #E3F0FF
  rectangle "2. system prompt — the rules\n(read everything, draft only, never say 'scheduled')" as s #E3F0FF
  rectangle "3. AssistantContext — this week, targets,\nnear plan items, next fortnight, recent days\n(no timestamp: 'today' is the only clock)" as c #E3F0FF
  rectangle "★ explicit breakpoint on the last system block\n   covers 1 + 2 + 3 — changes once per TURN" as bp1 #FFE7BA
  rectangle "4. conversation history — earlier turns" as h #E4F9EC
  rectangle "5. this turn's tool results so far\n(all of a round's results in ONE user message)" as r #E4F9EC
  rectangle "★ automatic top-level breakpoint\n   covers 4 + 5 — changes once per ROUND" as bp2 #FFE7BA
  rectangle "6. the newest message" as n #F0E9FF
}

t -down-> s
s -down-> c
c -down-> bp1
bp1 -down-> h
h -down-> r
r -down-> bp2
bp2 -down-> n

note right of bp1
  Opus 5 caches a prefix only from
  512 tokens up; the tools and context
  alone are past that. TTL five minutes,
  refreshed by every read — rounds are
  seconds apart.
end note

note right of bp2
  Why the same bytes matter: a clock
  reading in the context, or tools in a
  different order, would change segment
  3 and silently miss every read.
  ContextServiceTest guards it.
end note
@enduml
